{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>It is in the power of my hand.</b> The wicked boast of their evil deeds, and so Laban the wicked was boasting here of an evil deed. And what he said, <b>It is in the power of my hand</b>, is joined with <b>and the God of your father</b>. He said: It is in the power of my hand all the twenty years that you were in my house, to do harm to you, and nothing restrained me — for the God of your father never appeared to me until last night; and this is a sign that my intention toward you is not evil. But now that you departed on the pretext that you yearned for your father's house — why did you steal my gods? And the doubling of the expression in כ' (the letter) is to strengthen, that is to say, with an absolute statement." "<b>From good to evil.</b> His 'good' was evil in Jacob's eyes — for even if he intended to do him a kindness, namely, not to kill him but only to take of his goods, this was evil in Jacob's eyes; therefore he needed to say <b>from good to evil</b>. And like it (Ezekiel 21:8): ״והכרתי ממך צדיק ורשע צדיק לבעל ורשע לבעל״. And the proof for this interpretation is that Jacob said to him, ״לולי אלהי אבי״ etc., ״כי עתה ריקם שלחתני״ — for that is the 'good' you intended for me."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The reference to כ' (the letter kaf) likely points to the repeated phrasing in the verse; the precise grammatical referent is uncertain and rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:41.360659648Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}